# mbsync (isync) — pull IMAP into ~/mail as a Maildir.
#
# Symlinked to ~/.mbsyncrc by install.sh. Password comes from pass, which
# goes through gpg-agent / the Yubikey — see mail/README.md.
#
# Written for isync >= 1.4 (Arch ships 1.5.x): the keywords are Far/Near and
# TLSType. Most tutorials online still say Master/Slave and SSLType, which
# this version does not accept.

IMAPAccount holten
Host        imap.one.com              # IMAP/Incoming server
Port        993
User        joseph@holten.com
PassCmd     "pass mail/holten"
TLSType     IMAPS
CertificateFile /etc/ssl/certs/ca-certificates.crt

IMAPStore holten-remote
Account   holten

MaildirStore holten-local
Path       ~/mail/holten/
Inbox      ~/mail/holten/INBOX
SubFolders Verbatim

Channel holten
Far     :holten-remote:
Near    :holten-local:
Create  Near
SyncState *
# NOTE: no blank line may separate these from the Channel header above — in
# mbsync an empty line ends a section, and they would then be parsed as global
# keywords ("'Patterns' is not a recognized ... keyword").
#
# All folders except trash/spam. The duplicate pairs (Sent/"Sent Items",
# Trash/"Deleted Items", Spam/spam) are leftovers from an earlier mail client;
# the Sent pair is kept because both hold real mail.
Patterns * !Trash !"Trash/*" !"Deleted Items" !Spam !spam
# Sync defaults to All (two-way). Expunge defaults to None, so deleting mail
# locally flags it rather than removing it from the server — deliberate.

# --- KIT ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Second account. Its maildir sits beside holten's under ~/mail/, which is
# what makes both share one notmuch index (database.path is ~/mail, one level
# up — see notmuch-config).
#
# User is the KIT account name (np6630), not the address np6630@kit.edu.
# The credential is `pass kit`, a top-level entry — NOT mail/kit, unlike
# holten's. Host/Port are the SCC values; confirm with
#   mbsync -l kit
# which lists folders and transfers nothing.

IMAPAccount kit
Host        imap.kit.edu
Port        993
User        np6630
PassCmd     "pass kit"
# Required. AuthMechs defaults to `*`, and KIT's Exchange server advertises
# NTLM, which mbsync then prefers over LOGIN — and it fails with
#   AUTHENTICATE NTLM ... returned an error: AUTHENTICATE failed
# Pinning LOGIN skips SASL negotiation entirely. Safe here because the whole
# session is already inside TLS (IMAPS on 993), so the password is never on
# the wire in the clear.
AuthMechs   LOGIN
# Also required. With pipelining on, this server produces
#   IMAP error: malformed FETCH response ...: unexpected attribute
# partway through the first real sync. mbsync(1) documents PipelineDepth as
# existing partly "to spare flaky servers like M$ Exchange" — 1 disables
# pipelining entirely. Slower, but it is the difference between syncing and
# not. Do not raise it without re-testing a full `mbsync -V kit`.
PipelineDepth 1
TLSType     IMAPS
CertificateFile /etc/ssl/certs/ca-certificates.crt

IMAPStore kit-remote
Account   kit

MaildirStore kit-local
Path       ~/mail/kit/
Inbox      ~/mail/kit/INBOX
SubFolders Verbatim

Channel kit
Far     :kit-remote:
Near    :kit-local:
Create  Near
SyncState *
# Exchange, German locale, so none of holten's English exclusions match here —
# the names come from `mbsync -l kit`, don't guess them. Beyond trash/junk this
# also drops the non-mail Exchange folders (calendar, contacts, tasks, notes,
# journal): they are not mail, notmuch cannot index them usefully, and
# Postausgang/Outbox is a send queue that has no business being mirrored.
# (one line — mbsyncrc has no backslash line-continuation)
# There are TWO trash folders (Exchange's "Gelöschte Elemente" and a plain
# "Trash" left by some earlier client) and likewise two Sent — same duplication
# holten has. Both trashes are dropped; both Sents are kept, since either may
# hold real mail.
Patterns * !"Gelöschte Elemente" !Trash !"Trash/*" !"Junk-E-Mail" !Postausgang !Kalender !"Kalender/*" !Kontakte !Aufgaben !Notizen !Journal

# A group, so one name syncs everything. `mbsync all` == `mbsync -a` here, but
# naming it keeps on-new-mail.sh and any manual run pointed at one thing.
# NOTE: adding a future account means adding it here too.
Group all
Channel holten
Channel kit
